1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,768
This sandwich used to be for RICH PEOPLE.

2
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:07,829
Yeah. THAT sandwich. The one your mom threw in a plastic bag.

3
00:00:10,250 --> 00:00:16,844
Back in the late 1800s, peanut butter wasn’t even considered normal food. It was promoted as a health food!

4
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:24,420
And somehow, it became a delicacy for wealthy Americans. They were eating it with watercress, cheese, even PIMENTOS.

5
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:27,029
Clearly, somebody needed to intervene.

6
00:00:28,350 --> 00:00:30,068
Enter Julia Davis Chandler.

7
00:00:31,150 --> 00:00:36,008
In 1901, she published the earliest known recipe combining peanut paste with jelly.

8
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:40,447
Not grape jelly. Not strawberry. CURRANT or CRABAPPLE jelly.

9
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:43,579
But she was onto something.

10
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:50,495
Then the 1920s happened. Peanut butter got creamier, lasted longer, and became cheaper.

11
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,483
And somebody finally figured out how to sell bread PRE-SLICED.

12
00:00:55,700 --> 00:01:01,133
Suddenly, you didn’t need a fancy tea room. You needed three ingredients and about thirty seconds.

13
00:01:03,100 --> 00:01:08,149
Then World War Two came along. Peanut butter and jelly were both included in American military rations.

14
00:01:08,650 --> 00:01:13,601
Cheap. Portable. Packed with energy. And when soldiers came home, the combination was already familiar.

15
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:16,675
Before long, PB&J was taking over American lunchboxes.

16
00:01:17,100 --> 00:01:22,201
So this ridiculously simple sandwich went from fancy tea parties… to wartime rations… to practically every American childhood.

17
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:25,486
All because someone looked at peanut butter and thought…

18
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:28,508
You know what this needs? Fruit goo.
